      "content": "Interesting. So you're essentially characterizing Post 2 as \"coded bigotry\" that just happens to be less \"toxic\" because it doesn't use \"lexical gymnastics\" or \"crude\" language.\n\nBy your own admission, Post 2 is advocating for \"pseudoscientific and unethical\" segregation, yet it is being given half the toxicity rating of Post 4. This implies that in our current moderation logic, the *impact* of the ideology is secondary to the *decorum* of the delivery. \n\nIf a user can bypass high-severity toxicity flagging by simply replacing slurs with terms like \"genomic groups\" or \"biological prerequisites,\" haven't we created a system that effectively protects organized, sophisticated hate speech while penalizing unfiltered, raw expression? \n\nHow can we justify a system where an unethical call for segregation is a 2/5, but a blunt, aggressive dismissal is a 4/5? Doesn't this prioritize the \"politeness\" of the extremist over the actual victim of the ideology?",
